I’ll inspect the pattern metadata, the frozen powder-XRD workflow, and available refinement backends first. Then I’ll build a phase inventory, test plausible structures under a common instrumental/profile model, and validate that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and `final.gpx` all come from the same replayed refinement.

The chemistry is a 500 °C Mn₂O₃ + WO₃ reaction, so I’m treating Mn–W–O products, both precursors, and plausible manganese/tungsten intermediates as candidates—not assuming the nominal MnWO₄ product is the only phase. The scan has a substantial low-angle background, so phase screening will use background-subtracted peak positions and coherent reflection families before any abundance judgment.

The peak inventory already separates into three coherent sets: monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ dominates the 23–24.7° triplet and its split higher-angle families; bixbyite Mn₂O₃ accounts for 33.27, 38.53, 49.60, 55.49, and 66.03°; weak features near 15.83, 18.74, and 30.26° make MnWO₄ a live minor-phase candidate. I’m now testing alternate WO₃ and Mn₂O₃ records under the same restrained background, angular correction, and profile so a source-record mismatch is not mistaken for absence.

The initial common-profile fit reaches about 8.35% Rwp with WO₃ + Mn₂O₃, while MnWO₄ is driven to essentially zero scale and destabilizes its cell if allowed to refine. I’m not accepting that zero-scale result as definitive yet: the current WO₃ record/profile may be absorbing weak intensity, so I’m completing the alternate-record screen before re-challenging MnWO₄ and Mn₃WO₆ under the clean profile.

The physically constrained replay is stable: fixing a modest positive U/W/X baseline and introducing isotropic size and strain phase-by-phase reproduces the same ~7.00% Rwp without negative profile terms. Both phases require finite size (about 0.14 µm in the current partition); WO₃ clearly benefits from strain, while the Mn₂O₃ strain term is weak and will be pruned unless it remains useful after structural refinement. I’m keeping the common X term fixed because releasing it is nearly singular with crystallite size.

The common intensity trend is real: polarization plus a modest Suortti low-angle roughness correction lowers Rwp from 6.89% to 6.42% without bound hits; using specimen displacement instead of the established zero correction does not help. Texture trials collapse close to random powder and are being rejected. In contrast, WO₃ shows replay-stable, strongly significant uniaxial microstrain along its monoclinic b direction (about 3.10 vs 5.21×10³ ppm axial/equatorial), reducing Rwp to 6.23%; Mn₂O₃ anisotropy is unsupported.

The clean-model watch-list challenge finds no defensible Mn₃WO₆ (≤0.08 wt% before its cell drifts) and rejects Mn₃O₄: its nominal ~0.23 wt% “improvement” requires an implausible 15% cell-volume expansion. MnWO₄ is different: several weak families give a positive ~0.9 wt% scale and lower Rwp from 6.23% to 6.17%, but the sole exact COD record needs a ~1.3% a-axis change. I’m therefore treating MnWO₄ as provisional and testing physical wolframite analogues before deciding whether its structure/fraction is reliable enough to submit.

The phase inventory is now coherent: monoclinic WO₃ and bixbyite Mn₂O₃ are the major phases, with a weak but reproducible MnWO₄ contribution. I’m doing the final full-range replay and export checks now; the main remaining issue is quantifying how model-dependent that trace MnWO₄ fraction is.

The current residuals are dominated by imperfect peak-shape/intensity agreement at the strongest WO₃ families, not by a coherent set of unassigned peaks. I’m therefore retaining the three-phase inventory and will report MnWO₄ as provisional, with a wider model-based fraction range than its formal least-squares uncertainty.

I’ve frozen the accepted atomic coordinates after their staged refinement and reconverged only the cells, scales, background, and supported profile terms. This removes the near-singular paired-W coordinate directions while preserving the refined structure; the fit changes by less than 0.001 percentage point in Rwp.

The final project now passes independent atomic forward replay on the complete measured grid, with no negative profile widths, background, or displacement-tensor flags. The three CIFs also pass composition, density, occupancy, and short-contact checks, and their structures/fractions match the saved GSAS-II project.

Completed the PXRD analysis.

- WO₃, P2₁/c: 74.435 wt%
- Mn₂O₃, Ia-3: 24.641 wt%
- MnWO₄, P2/c: 0.924 wt% — provisional; profile-model range 0.28–1.43 wt%
- Final Rwp: 6.1633%; GOF: 2.3186

The full-range GSAS-II atomic replay and CIF physical-screen checks passed.

